    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The OECD suggests that young people, ages 18-25, will be the hardest hit by the future of work as entry-level positions are more likely to involve the routine tasks with low skills requirements most at risk of disruptions or transition (OECD, 2018). A lack of social capital and exposure to careers prevent them from finding the necessary support to thrive in this new environment. These underserved youth need support to translate their skills and experiences into competencies relevant to the future of work. \r\n\r\nPeopleMap is a web application that creates visualizations of the skills in learners’ resumes and LinkedIn profiles to maps them the Institute for the Future's Future of Work Competency framework (IFTF, 2018). \r\n\r\nThis approach was taken to address a learning problem, as studies by Schwartz et al. have shown that self-explanation and visualizations are powerful strategies to learn more deeply (Schwartz et al., 2016).\r\n\r\nPreliminary learner tests show that when learners use PeopleMap, they increase the number of skills used to self-describe. These early results show that PeopleMap is a tool for promoting self-explanation, in which students are able to quickly construct an understanding of their skills, rather than just memorization.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Maples, Bethanie. Rosas, Sergio. Forshaw, Tessa. (2018). PeopleMap: Closing the Signaling Gap. Masters Project Report. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at: https://purl.stanford.edu/kf926bk2858",
            "type": "preferred citation",
